==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|browsen}}, from {{der|en|fro|brouster}}, {{m|fro|broster||to nibble off buds, sprouts, and bark; browse}}, from {{m|fro|brost||a sprout, shoot, bud}}, from a {{der|en|gem}} source, perhaps {{der|en|frk|*brust||shoot, bud}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*brustiz||bud, shoot}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰrews-||to swell, sprout}}. Cognate with {{cog|bar|Bross}}, {{m|bar|Brosst||a bud}}, {{cog|osx|brustian||to sprout}}. {{doublet|en|brut|breast|brush}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɹaʊz/|/bɹæʊz/}} 
* {{audio|en|en-us-browse.ogg|a=US}}
* {{homophones|en|brows}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊz|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[scan]], to casually [[look]] through in order to find items of interest, especially without [[knowledge]] of what to look for [[beforehand]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm just '''browsing''' around.}}
#: {{ux|en|I stopped in several bookstores '''to browse'''.}}
#* {{RQ:James International Episode|passage=At Hampton Court the little flocks of visitors are not provided with an official bellwether, but are left to '''browse''' at discretion upon the local antiquities.}}
#* {{RQ:Morley Haunted Bookshop|passage=The little room in which he found himself was plainly the bookseller's sanctum, and contained his own private library.  Gilbert '''browsed''' along the shelves curiously. The volumes were mostly shabby and bruised; they had evidently been picked up one by one in the humble mangers of the second-hand vendor.}}
# To move about while sampling, such as with food or products on display.
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To [[navigate]] through [[hyperlinked]] [[document]]s on a [[computer]], usually with a [[browser]].
#* {{quote-web|en|date=1990-11-12|author=w:Tim Berners-Lee; w:Robert Cailliau|url=http://www.w3.org/Proposal.html|title=WorldWideWeb: Proposal for a HyperText Project|work=World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)|passage=HyperText is a way to link and access information of various kinds as a web of nodes in which the user can '''browse''' at will.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260913142332/https://www.w3.org/Proposal.html|archivedate=13 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of an animal}} To move about while eating parts of plants, especially plants other than [[pasture]], such as [[shrub]]s or [[tree]]s.
#* {{RQ:Grey Desert|passage=Sheep ranged everywhere under the low cedars. They '''browsed''' with noses in the frost, and from all around came the tinkle of tiny bells on the curly-horned rams, and an endless variety of bleats.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|year=1997|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20100611165037/http://csfs.colostate.edu/pdfs/wildlife_values.pdf|work=Colorado State Forest Service|title=Wildlife Values of Conservation|author=Kent Grant|passage=Also, when planting to provide a source of browse for wintering deer and elk, protect seedlings from '''browsing''' during the first several years; an electric fence enclosure can offer effective protection.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[feed on]], as [[pasture]]; to pasture on; to [[graze]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=Gardener's Daughter|page=21|passage=The fields between / Are dewy-fresh, '''brows'd''' by deep-udder'd kine, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|passage=“If fifteen cows is '''browsing''' on a hillside, how many of them eats with their heads pointed the same direction?”}}

